# A channel bound to a SECOND name. Accepted until 2026-08-14 (FRICTION #45).
#
# No double free comes of it -- the free is keyed to the creating declaration --
# but the alias defeats the compiler's own analysis: `send(e, 41)` is not counted
# as a send on `c`, so this program, which sends on every path, still drew
# "nothing ever sends on channel 'c', so a receive on it parks forever". A
# warning that is wrong about the code in front of it is worse than none.
#
# The spec already said `channel(T, cap)` is legal only as a declaration's direct
# right-hand side (docs/spec/13-concurrency.md:176); nothing enforced it. The two
# siblings -- a task, a typed handle -- refuse the same shape.
# expect: a channel cannot be copied
# expect: hides every send and receive from the compiler's analysis

fn main():
    c := channel(int, 2)
    e := c
    send(e, 41)
    println("got=" + str(recv(c)))
